@* QrCode's Overview, written by hand: a link, two-factor setup (previewed in each mode), size and
   error correction. Accessibility and See also come from the doc comment. *@

<TextBlock>A QR code shows text, such as a link, for a phone's camera to read. It renders on the server as an SVG, so it works with <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">JavaScript off</Anchor>, scales without blurring, and prints.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>It's dark on light in both color schemes, since not every scanner reads light on dark. For a product or shipping number read by a barcode scanner, see <Anchor Href="docs/components/barcode">Barcode</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">A link to scan</Heading>
<TextBlock>Set <Code>Value</Code> to the text, and <Code>Label</Code> to what scanning it does, such as "QR code for this page". Screen readers hear the label, never the value. Beside the code, write what a user without a camera needs, such as the link.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="qr-code-usage" />

<Heading Level="2">Setting up two-factor sign-in</Heading>
<TextBlock>An authenticator app scans an <Code>otpauth://totp/</Code> link that contains a secret key, then shows a new six-digit code every 30 seconds. Show the key beside the QR code, in groups of four, for a user who types it in instead. Then ask for the first code in a <Anchor Href="docs/components/pin-field">PinField</Anchor>, to confirm the app has the key.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="qr-code-two-factor" />
<TextBlock>The link contains the secret, so don't log it or put it in a URL. The link is <Code>otpauth://totp/Issuer:account?secret=KEY&amp;issuer=Issuer</Code>, with your site's name as the issuer in both places and the issuer and account URL-encoded.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>With ASP.NET Core Identity, <Code>UserManager.ResetAuthenticatorKeyAsync</Code> makes the key, <Code>GetAuthenticatorKeyAsync</Code> reads it, and <Code>VerifyTwoFactorTokenAsync</Code>, with the authenticator token provider, checks the code. A real check also rejects a code that was already used, so a code seen over a shoulder can't be used again.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Size and error correction</Heading>
<TextBlock>To set the width and height, set <Code>Size</Code>; it's 10rem by default, and the code scales to fit. Scanners look for the light border around the code, 4 modules (squares) wide by default (<Code>QuietZone</Code>), so keep it. Longer text makes a denser code with smaller modules, so keep links short.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock><Code>ErrorCorrection</Code> sets how much of the code can be damaged and still scan: <Code>Medium</Code> by default, about 15%. A higher level makes the code denser. When a higher level fits in the same size, it's used anyway.</TextBlock>

